WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.294 --> 00:00:05.506
When you're working with materials
coming from Stephen King's mind,

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.132
it's a huge challenge.

00:00:07.508 --> 00:00:12.638
It was a very long process of
investigation, designing, discovering,

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.599
because it has to be beautiful,
it has to be mysterious.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.227
We wanted to create a world
that we have not seen before.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.944
Design in feature films and television
is a very lengthy process

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:30.364
of back and forth with
the Director and the creator.

00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:33.075
It's an epic undertaking.

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:37.871
Pablo and Stephen King
wanted this to feel stylized.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.541
Everything looks real,

00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:42.668
everything looks unreal,

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:43.961
-at the same time.
-Yeah.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.675
So much of this is experimental.

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:52.302
We just had to kind of design as we went.

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:58.725
I read the novel first, and then,
obviously, I read your script treatment.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:00.352
And it was interesting to see

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:03.939
your interpretation
from the novel to the script 

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:06.650
and the details that you wanted
to keep in the script,

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:07.943
and the stuff that you didn't.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.488
One of the things that I did was
I thought to myself,

00:01:12.114 --> 00:01:16.827
"This is a place where it's always twilight,
it's always kind of a mystery.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:20.706
This is a place
that's outside of time, kind of."

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:22.374
And that's the way that you built it.

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.630
Mystery is not only in how it looks.

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:31.925
It can also be in the way that it's shot.

00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:35.220
When Pablo talked to me
about Boo'ya Moon, it was difficult.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.639
When you read Boo'ya Moon,
if you don't know anything,

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.974
how would you do Boo'ya Moon?

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:44.271
So the concept was born between
Pablo, and then Guy, and then me.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.899
And then we just all
had this interpretation,

00:01:46.982 --> 00:01:49.902
and then Boo'ya Moon became
Pablo's Boo'ya Moon.

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:55.157
Darius really knows how to create
suspense with his images,

00:01:55.324 --> 00:01:57.910
how to create
things that can be unsettling.

00:01:59.745 --> 00:02:01.455
I'll take you there again.

00:02:01.622 --> 00:02:02.247
Yeah.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.959
When you read it, you think,
"How are they going to create this world?"

00:02:06.043 --> 00:02:09.171
It's literally complete epic otherworldly.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.548
Pablo Larraín is a tremendous director.

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:13.884
Darius Khondji is one of the
best cinematographers in the world.

00:02:14.218 --> 00:02:16.303
It's so, so beautiful.

00:02:17.304 --> 00:02:21.517
It's a fantastic opportunity
to recreate what you imagine

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.896
Stephen King had in mind when
he was writing this extraordinary story.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.692
It was so beautifully rendered, this world.

00:02:31.485 --> 00:02:34.029
I can't say enough about
the entire production.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:38.283
It's a narrative that comes from
Stephen King's world

00:02:38.951 --> 00:02:42.246
that was embraced by
all these incredible artists and producers.

00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:45.082
And I hope the audience will
connect with it

00:02:45.457 --> 00:02:46.542
in the most beautiful way.

